"Flood maps showing water depths in the event of flooding are crucial for flood risk management and crisis management. While risk management and crisis management are both essential for mitigating the risk of extreme events, they have different focuses. Risk management focuses on identifying, assessing, and mitigating potential hazards before they occur, and thus aims to prevent crises. Crisis management, on the other hand, focuses on responding to and managing an impending event, as well as dealing with an event that has just occurred.\n\nWhile maximum flood inundation maps are essential for flood risk management, additional information on the temporal sequence is crucial for crisis management. Computationally intensive, physics-based models are the preferred flood simulation tool for risk management. However, due to the high computing time they require, physics-based models for crisis management are only acceptable with the help of high-performance computers (HPC). To counteract this disadvantage, scientists are researching data-driven, multi-stage flood prediction systems based on artificial intelligence (AI) that can be run on any personal computer.\n\nIn this study, we present several forecasting methods for flood prediction based on physically based models, and the latest developments applying AI. The latter include AI methods that, trained on physically based models, make use of convolutional neural networks (CNN), artificial neural networks (ANN), physically informed neural networks (PINNs), and using either recursive or sequential networks, among others. All of which can forecast flood inundation in a matter of seconds."
